
Hard Link : A hard link is another filename pointing to the same inode/data as the original file.
=========

# Create it with:
ln original.txt hardlink.txt

# Conceptually:
              ┌── original.txt
Same inode ───┤
              └── hardlink.txt
Both filenames refer to the same actual file data.


# Check:
ls -li

# Example:
12345 -rw-r--r-- 2 user user 100 Aug 16 original.txt
12345 -rw-r--r-- 2 user user 100 Aug 16 hardlink.txt

Notice: 12345 is the same inode number. And the link count is: 2 because two filenames point to that inode.


Important Characteristics:
=========================
Can link to files only (normally cannot be used for directories)
Cannot point to a file on a different filesystem
Does not have its own inode — it shares the same inode as the original file
If the original filename is deleted, the hard link still works
Multiple hard links can point to the same inode
Changes made through one hard link are visible through all other hard links
The file's data is removed only when all hard links are deleted and no process still has the file open
Hard links generally cannot be created for symbolic links; ln normally creates a hard link to the symlink's target inode rather than making a hard link to the symlink itself
Hard links must be on the same filesystem as the original file
